<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zwide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1758–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Zwide kaLanga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zwide was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> in the late 18th and early 19th centuries. He killed the Mthethwa leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> in about 1818 and then fought Shaka's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, who defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mhlatuze-river">Mhlatuze River</a> in 1819. The break-up of his kingdom sent his commanders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwangendaba">Zwangendaba</a>, north to found new states.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zwide was the son of Langa KaXaba, a king of the Nxumalo, and ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> (Nxumalo) nation from about 1805 to around 1820. At the time he came to the throne the Ndwandwe were growing in military strength, and Zwide, eager to extend their supremacy, became the chief rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a>, king of the Mthethwa, and of Dingiswayo's general and protégé Shaka kaSenzangakhona, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Besides breaking the power of the Mthethwa Kingdom, Zwide fought the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gqokli-hill">Battle of Gqokli Hill</a>. He also overran the neighbouring Khumalo Kingdom and executed its king, Matshobana KaMangete. Matshobana's son and heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a>, escaped and took refuge with Shaka, who had rebuilt the remnant of the Mthethwa under his own rule.</p><p>Knowing this, Zwide planned to destroy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> and secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> dominance over Zululand. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mhlatuze-river">Mhlatuze River</a> his army was caught halfway across when the Zulu attacked, and it was scattered. Zwide escaped with a remnant of his people across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pongola-river">Pongola River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After his flight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> fell on the rest of his people, killing many at Mome Gorge, a desolate place, and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> capital, KwaNongoma. Shaka's victory gave the impression of being the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>, the great scattering of peoples, although Zwide's own wars had already scattered others before it. His generals fled north and founded kingdoms of their own, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a> established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zwide kaLanga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressed by the expanding Ndwandwe Kingdom of <b>Zwide</b> in the late 1810s, he led his people north into the hills of central Eswatini and settled in the Ezulwini valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The region was dominated by two larger powers, the Mthethwa Paramountcy of Dingiswayo and the Ndwandwe Kingdom of <b>Zwide</b>, competing for land, cattle and control of trade with the Portuguese at Delagoa Bay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ntombazi, mother of the Ndwandwe king <b>Zwide</b>, was a sangoma, a diviner, and Shaka had her killed.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Soshangane, a commander of <b>Zwide's</b> Ndwandwe Kingdom, fled north after the Ndwandwe defeat by the Zulu and by about 1824 had settled in southern Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1818 Dingiswayo was captured and killed by <b>Zwide</b> of the Ndwandwe Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/mthethwa-paramountcy">Mthethwa Paramountcy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Langa and especially his son <b>Zwide</b> in the late 18th and early 19th centuries, they expanded through conquest into a major power.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Nongoma, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27.88,31.63&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Nongoma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Zwide"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5123 4820h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5123 4820h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-27.88,31.63&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
